{; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; THE BLOCK — the one mechanism behind every page and every prompt.
 ;
 ; Owner ruling, 2026-07-27 night, binding on N4: the root interface "is
 ; really just different context blocks that return :seon.render/ai and
 ; :seon.render/html", so blocks are central and root and agent views are
 ; ONE mechanism. Root is per-cluster and root is an agent, so "root's
 ; page" is not a second code path — it is the root agent's block set.
 ;
 ; A block is a UNIT for the landed router (src/seon/render.clj): a map
 ; declaring, per output kind, the qualified symbol that projects it.
 ; That is why this file registers no renderer table, no page registry
 ; and no dispatch: `:seon.render/html` below is one more kind key on an
 ; open set, exactly as `:seon.render/log` was, and `seon.render/render`
 ; needs no edit to route a block.
 ;
 ; File boundaries are editorial (the loader is one global population),
 ; so the `:seon.render/html` kind is declared HERE, in the file that
 ; owns its first consumer, rather than by editing the sealed
 ; render.edn — the same reason error.edn owns nothing about the router.
 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

 ; THE NAME IN THREE ROLES, and always in sync because there is only one
 ; of it: the prompt header the agent reads, the upsert key within one
 ; agent's block set, and (through `seon.render.block/surface-id`) the
 ; DOM element id the morph targets. That third role is what makes "the
 ; agent edits the same thing the human sees" true rather than a slogan.
 ;
 ; A plain `:keyword`, deliberately NOT a database identity: two agents
 ; each own a `:transcript` block, and making the name unique across the
 ; store would forbid exactly the arrangement the design requires.
 ; Uniqueness is per agent and is enforced by the upsert, not by Datahike.
 :seon.render.block/name :keyword

 ; ORDER IS DERIVED FROM DATA, never from position. Cardinality-many is a
 ; SET in Datahike (persistent_set.cljc:133), so a block set has no
 ; stored order to lose; the render sorts by priority with the name as a
 ; stable tiebreaker, which makes two derivations of one database value
 ; the same value. Lower sorts first, so a header can sit at 0 without
 ; anybody renumbering the rest.
 :seon.render.block/priority :int

 ; The semantic cache band, AUTHORED like priority: an installer's
 ; ordering decision, not a derived classification (no name→band table
 ; anywhere — R34). Absent = :dynamic, the free tail, so most blocks
 ; declare nothing. Ordering is (band ordinal, priority, name); the band
 ; order is the enum's own order and bands never cross.
 :seon.render.block/band [:enum :anchor :program :authored :continuity :dynamic]

 ; DECLARED trusted runtime inputs — the request keys this block's
 ; projections read beyond the database value. The interface expresses
 ; its dependency (the timeouts-ruling corollary): the request builder
 ; refuses AT CONSTRUCTION when a membership needs an absent input,
 ; before any projection runs, instead of every consumer growing its own
 ; fence card. Post-N5 this becomes derivable from the program graph;
 ; the attribute stays as the declaration the derivation must match.
 :seon.context/inputs [:set :qualified-keyword]

 ; THE BLOCK ENTITY. `:seon.db/entity true` is load-bearing rather than
 ; decorative: `canonical-database-attributes` installs entity-map
 ; entries by construction, and a family declared only as standalone
 ; attributes is unavailable to the LIVE boot path even while fixtures
 ; that install explicit lists stay green — the fixture-vs-live-boot
 ; class that already bit the message family (38ab48470).
 ;
 ; Both render keys are OPTIONAL and presence decides placement, which is
 ; the whole selection mechanism: ai only = prompt only (no surface),
 ; html only = a surface only (zero prompt tokens), both = both. A block
 ; carrying neither is legal and renders nowhere — it is data the agent
 ; owns and nothing displays, and refusing it would be inventing a rule
 ; the design does not need.
 :seon.render.block/block
 [:map {:seon.db/entity true}
  [:seon.render.block/name :seon.render.block/name]
  [:seon.render.block/priority :seon.render.block/priority]
  [:seon.render/ai {:optional true} :seon.render/ai]
  [:seon.render/html {:optional true} :seon.render/html]
  [:seon.render.block/band {:optional true} :seon.render.block/band]
  [:seon.context/inputs {:optional true} :seon.context/inputs]]

 ; EACH AGENT OWNS ITS COMPLETE SET, seeded at creation. There is no
 ; render-time merge with a default set and no fallback: every block an
 ; agent renders, it owns, so "what does this agent see?" is one pull
 ; rather than a reconstruction. Component, so removing the agent
 ; cascade-retracts its blocks and no orphan block outlives its owner.
 :seon.cluster.agent/blocks [:set {:seon.db/component true} :seon.db/ref]

 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; THE HTML KIND
 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

 ; One more kind on the open set. Its consumer is a surface in the
 ; browser, and its output grammar is hiccup — stated by the consumer,
 ; per the router's rule that a kind's grammar belongs to the kind's
 ; consumer and never to the router.
 :seon.render/html :seon.render/projection

 ; HICCUP IS A NAMED PREDICATE, and that is a limitation being paid for
 ; honestly rather than a preference. Hiccup is a tree, and a
 ; self-referential declaration currently overflows the stack in
 ; `seon.schema/bind-predicates` (issue
 ; a-self-referential-schema-overflows-the-stack). The supported idiom
 ; for a shape this registry cannot express is a registered core
 ; predicate with an honest generator, which is strictly better than
 ; `:any` here anyway: the grammar belongs to the serializer that
 ; enforces it, the predicate IS that grammar, and the generator's
 ; output domain is the serializer's input domain so every totality
 ; property generates against the real thing.
 :seon.render/hiccup [:fn {:gen/gen seon.render.hiccup/hiccup-generator} seon.render.hiccup/hiccup?]

 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; THE RENDERED PAGE
 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

 ; The DOM element id, derived from the name by ONE function so the hole
 ; and the patch can never drift apart. `surface-` prefixed because the
 ; id shares a document with ids nobody here owns.
 :seon.render/surface-id [:string {:min 9}]

 ; ONE RENDERED BLOCK. Success carries the output; failure carries the
 ; flat error value INSTEAD — never merged beside it, because
 ; `:seon.error/value` is a closed shape registered once and a map that
 ; is both is neither. The surface always carries its name and id, so a
 ; failed block still has a place to put its error card and its
 ; neighbours are unaffected: per-block isolation is structural here, not
 ; a try/catch somebody remembered.
 ;
 ; A UNION OF TWO CLOSED MAPS rather than one map with two optional keys,
 ; and no discriminator: closedness already makes the two disjoint, and
 ; `:seon.error/value` presence is the same "did this render?" test the
 ; router's own wrap contract established. This is not the `:close`
 ; precedent — there the FOLD's end had to be visible in the value
 ; because an absent ordinal meant two different things; here nothing is
 ; inferred from an absence, only read from a presence.
 :seon.render/surface
 [:or
  [:map {:closed true}
   [:seon.render.block/name :seon.render.block/name]
   [:seon.render/surface-id :seon.render/surface-id]
   [:seon.render/kind :seon.render/kind]
   [:seon.render/output :seon.render/output]]
  [:map {:closed true}
   [:seon.render.block/name :seon.render.block/name]
   [:seon.render/surface-id :seon.render/surface-id]
   [:seon.render/kind :seon.render/kind]
   [:seon.error/value :seon.error/value]]]

 ; The agent's blocks rendered into one kind, in priority order. A vector
 ; because the ORDER is the meaning; the set it came from had none.
 :seon.render/surfaces [:vector :seon.render/surface]

 ; What a caller asks for. The database value is a positional argument
 ; rather than a key, matching the landed `seon.problems/problems`
 ; signature — one convention for "pure over a database value", not two.
 ; caps + trusted inputs now ride the request — the ONE builder threads
 ; them into every projection (review finding 3).
 ; `:seon.ai/partial` is the TRANSIENT stream snapshot (F2 §2): the
 ; render pass threads the channel-borne presentation value through the
 ; one unit builder, so the streaming blocks read it exactly where every
 ; block reads its inputs — never a query, never a fact.
 :seon.render/surfaces-request
 [:map {:closed true}
  [:seon.cluster.agent/id :seon.cluster.agent/id]
  [:seon.render/kind :seon.render/kind]
  [:seon.sci.admit/caps :seon.sci.admit/caps]
  [:seon.ai/partial {:optional true} :seon.ai/partial]
  [:seon.cluster.run/live-processes {:optional true}
   :seon.cluster.run/live-processes]]

 ; the unit builder's input — one shape for prompt, page, debug, capture
 :seon.render/unit-request
 [:map {:closed true}
  [:seon.db/db :any]
  [:seon.cluster.agent/id :seon.cluster.agent/id]
  [:seon.sci.admit/caps :seon.sci.admit/caps]
  [:seon.ai/partial {:optional true} :seon.ai/partial]
  [:seon.cluster.run/live-processes {:optional true}
   :seon.cluster.run/live-processes]]

 ; What one bounded expansion needs. The database value is OPTIONAL
 ; because block-slot expansion needs none; an entity ref without one is
 ; refused in place, naming what it needed, rather than silently
 ; rendering an empty hole.
 :seon.render/expansion
 [:map {:closed true}
  [:seon.render/surfaces :seon.render/surfaces]
  [:seon.sci.admit/caps :seon.sci.admit/caps]
  [:seon.db/db {:optional true} :any]]

 ; What a page needs. Caps are REQUIRED, and they are the SAME four
 ; dials `seon.sci.admit` already takes — expansion is a walk over a
 ; graph that can fan out and can cycle, which is precisely the problem
 ; the admission codec already solved, so it uses that solution's dials
 ; rather than inventing a second set to drift from them.
 :seon.render/page-request
 [:map {:closed true}
  [:seon.cluster.agent/id :seon.cluster.agent/id]
  [:seon.sci.admit/caps :seon.sci.admit/caps]
  [:seon.cluster.run/live-processes {:optional true}
   :seon.cluster.run/live-processes]]

 ; A page is the html surfaces with every slot expanded, in the order
 ; the top-level blocks sort. Top-level is DERIVED (a block no other
 ; block's hiccup slots into), never a stored `:layout` flag — the
 ; layout-vs-surface distinction is a role the hiccup reveals.
 :seon.render/page [:vector :seon.render/hiccup]

 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ; GENERIC DEFAULT + SPECIALIST — the reusable selection shape
 ; (owner architectural ruling, 2026-07-27 night)
 ;
 ; Every output kind has a GENERIC default renderer that can project any
 ; value of the family. A producer that knows more about a particular
 ; value points that value's key at a SPECIALIST instead — and the
 ; choice is made WHERE THE UNIT IS BUILT, computed from the value's own
 ; attributes.
 ;
 ; The two halves of the ruling are one design. Because the key on the
 ; unit is the whole answer, a CONSUMER never branches: it hands the
 ; unit to the one router and receives whatever the producer decided.
 ; That is what makes "consumers reach an error's renderings only
 ; through the router" enforceable rather than a convention — there is
 ; no second call site to reach for, because the specialist's name never
 ; leaves the producer.
 ;
 ; It also keeps hot reload: the specialist is a SYMBOL resolved late by
 ; the router on every render, so re-evaluating it changes the next
 ; render, exactly as the generic default does.
 ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

 ; ONE RULE: a pure predicate over the value's own attributes, and the
 ; projection to use when it accepts. Both are qualified symbols
 ; resolved late — a rule is as hot-reloadable as the renderer it picks.
 :seon.render/specialist [:tuple :qualified-symbol :seon.render/projection]

 ; What a producer asks for. `specialists` is ORDERED and first match
 ; wins, because "more specific" is a judgement its owner makes and not
 ; something a scoring function should guess. An empty vector is the
 ; ordinary case and yields the default, so a family with no specialists
 ; writes no special code.
 ;
 ; This is NOT a registry: the vector belongs to the ONE producer that
 ; owns the family, is built from that family's own attributes, and is
 ; never consulted by anybody rendering. A second family's rules are
 ; invisible here, which is the difference between a producer's rules
 ; and a dispatch table.
 :seon.render/selection
 [:map {:closed true}
  [:seon.render/kind :seon.render/kind]
  [:seon.render/default :seon.render/projection]
  [:seon.render/specialists [:vector :seon.render/specialist]]]}
